1
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:06,830
Hello everyone and welcome back to another lesson at EnglishPod.

2
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:07,530
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:08,030 --> 00:00:12,930
And my name is Catherine and in the spirit of Halloween, we have a Halloween lesson for you.

4
00:00:13,070 --> 00:00:13,950
Happy Halloween.

5
00:00:15,110 --> 00:00:15,610
It's Halloween.

6
00:00:16,170 --> 00:00:21,530
It's Halloween and this is an elementary level lesson so we're talking about some basic but important things today.

7
00:00:22,130 --> 00:00:22,390
Exactly.

8
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:27,960
So, we're not going to preview anything because the dialogue is pretty simple.

9
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:31,690
So why don't we listen to it for the first time and we'll be right back.

10
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:41,980
Trick or treat.

11
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:44,980
Tom, aren't you a little too old to be trick or treating?

12
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:46,540
What are you talking about?

13
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:48,160
Where's your Halloween spirit?

14
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:52,740
Didn't you ever dress up in a costume and go around the neighborhood trick or treating with your friends?

15
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:54,080
Of course I did.

16
00:00:54,400 --> 00:01:01,180
When I was 10, trick or treating is for kids plus I'm sure people think you're a kidnapper or something running around with kids at night.

17
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:01,740
Whatever.

18
00:01:02,420 --> 00:01:03,140
I'm going next door.

19
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:07,800
I heard Mrs. Robinson is giving out big bags of M&Ms.

20
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:14,410
Alright, we're back.

21
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:16,150
So trick or treating.

22
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:17,750
Ding dong.

23
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:18,510
Ding dong.

24
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:20,200
Trick or treat.

25
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:25,190
So, we're going to explain what trick or treating is just in a little bit.

26
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:31,110
But before we get into it, why don't we take a look at four important words in Language Takeaway.

27
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:34,380
Language Takeaway.

28
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:41,920
Well, the first one, Marco, is very important because it's the name of this special holiday that isn't celebrated everywhere.

29
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:46,560
It's only celebrated in some places and Americans, I think, love it above all else.

30
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:46,800
Right.

31
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:47,620
Yeah, Halloween.

32
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:52,320
And this actually comes from All Hallows Eve, right?

33
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:52,540
That's right.

34
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:55,540
So it's the night of the spirits of the dead.

35
00:01:56,040 --> 00:01:56,640
Right, exactly.

36
00:01:56,920 --> 00:01:58,960
So the day of the dead.

37
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:10,380
And as Catherine mentions, it's celebrated in different ways in different countries but I think probably the United States is one of the few where people dress up and go trick or treating.

38
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:12,200
That's right.

39
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:14,430
And Halloween is the last day of October.

40
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:16,310
So October 31st.

41
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:23,350
And it's a really fun holiday where mostly children dress up in costumes and they ask for candy.

42
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:24,840
All right.

43
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:26,850
So to dress up means to get in a costume?

44
00:02:27,630 --> 00:02:27,790
Right.

45
00:02:27,930 --> 00:02:32,390
Well, you could say dress up to mean a couple of different things but in this case, you dress up in a costume.

46
00:02:32,710 --> 00:02:42,610
So that means you change your clothes so that you're wearing a costume which is maybe a witch's costume or a vampire or something scary.

47
00:02:42,970 --> 00:02:46,030
So you would say, he dressed up as a vampire.

48
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:51,550
He dressed up as a vampire or they got dressed up for the wedding.

49
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:52,920
OK.

50
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:53,920
Very good.

51
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:54,310
So dress up.

52
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:59,200
And then we have the word kidnapper.

53
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:06,560
So the friend is afraid that his friend will look like a kidnapper if he goes out trick or treating with the kids.

54
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:09,810
And so a kidnapper comes from the word to take or to grab.

55
00:03:10,410 --> 00:03:11,310
So kid grabber.

56
00:03:11,730 --> 00:03:16,570
And that means that you're going to take children from the streets and steal them.

57
00:03:16,810 --> 00:03:16,990
Right.

58
00:03:17,350 --> 00:03:19,590
But you can kidnap an adult, right?

59
00:03:19,810 --> 00:03:20,850
So it's not only for kids.

60
00:03:21,210 --> 00:03:21,810
That's right.

61
00:03:22,050 --> 00:03:25,930
It's for any kind of person who you take without them wanting you to.

62
00:03:26,110 --> 00:03:27,510
So against their will.

63
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:28,960
OK.

64
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:29,960
Kidnapper.

65
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:30,960
And finally, we have M&Ms.

66
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:31,960
What are M&Ms?

67
00:03:31,960 --> 00:03:32,960
Not the singer, right?

68
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:33,960
M&Ms.

69
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:34,420
No.

70
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:36,720
M&Ms is spelled differently, the singer.

71
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:38,760
This is a delicious chocolate treat.

72
00:03:39,060 --> 00:03:39,820
It's a candy.

73
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:41,820
And it comes in big brown bags.

74
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:44,960
And if you want them with peanuts, they come in a yellow bag.

75
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:45,960
You know your M&Ms.

76
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:47,420
Oh man, I could tell you all the colors.

77
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:49,420
But M&Ms are really delicious.

78
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:52,240
And it's actually a brand, M&Ms.

79
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:53,240
It's a brand.

80
00:03:53,240 --> 00:03:54,240
Right.

81
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:54,980
So many people probably know it.

82
00:03:55,240 --> 00:03:58,340
But just in case, they're called M&Ms.

83
00:03:58,340 --> 00:03:59,340
Very good candy.

84
00:03:59,340 --> 00:04:00,340
All right.

85
00:04:00,340 --> 00:04:02,920
So these words were relatively easy and simple.

86
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:05,680
So why don't we listen to our dialogue again.

87
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:07,480
Let's slow it down just a little bit.

88
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:10,100
And we'll be right back to take a look at Fluency Builder.

89
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:12,330
Fluency Builder.

90
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:14,080
All right.

91
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:15,080
We're back.

92
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:17,520
So on Fluency Builder, we promised that we were going to look at this word trick-or-treating.

93
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:18,570
So let's look at it now.

94
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:20,400
Trick-or-treat.

95
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:21,020
It's a verb, right?

96
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:22,400
To trick-or-treat.

97
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:23,760
To trick-or-treat.

98
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:29,360
And it comes from the words that we say when we're children to the person whose door we knock on.

99
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:31,680
So for example, I say...

100
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:33,440
And then I open the door.

101
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:35,720
I say trick-or-treat.

102
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:36,720
Okay.

103
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:40,280
So what are you supposed to do in this when somebody says trick-or-treat?

104
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:41,400
Well, I'm a child.

105
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:42,400
I'm holding my bag.

106
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:45,960
And you're supposed to say, happy Halloween and give me some candy.

107
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:46,700
Okay.

108
00:04:47,220 --> 00:04:47,460
All right.

109
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:51,600
So trick-or-treat means you could either trick someone.

110
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:55,920
You could play a trick on them like scare them or put some toilet paper on their house.

111
00:04:56,380 --> 00:04:57,760
Or you can give them a treat.

112
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:00,160
Or accept a treat.

113
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:04,320
And so the idea is if you don't give me a treat, I will do something bad to you.

114
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:05,120
I see.

115
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:06,900
But this doesn't really happen, right?

116
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:12,480
Like when kids go to people's houses, they say trick-or-treat and then they get candy automatically.

117
00:05:12,860 --> 00:05:14,320
Almost automatically, yeah.

118
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:19,200
Sometimes people avoid the holiday and they close their doors and lock them and they don't answer the door.

119
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:23,640
And that's when someone maybe throws an egg at the house or does something mean...

120
00:05:23,640 --> 00:05:25,680
Or puts toilet paper on their tree.

121
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:26,680
Yeah.

122
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:29,360
So trick-or-treat is what we say, but it's also something we can do.

123
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:30,760
So we can go trick-or-treating.

124
00:05:30,760 --> 00:05:31,760
Go trick-or-treating.

125
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:32,100
Right.

126
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:33,760
Okay.

127
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:34,760
Very good.

128
00:05:34,760 --> 00:05:35,760
Very good.

129
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:40,160
And now we have another phrase here that's a little bit complicated.

130
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:44,720
But when the friend answered the door, he said, aren't you a little too old to be trick-or-treating?

131
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:45,700
All right.

132
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:47,320
I like this phrase because it's sarcastic.

133
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:50,480
The person is not asking a real question.

134
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:54,680
He's saying, listen, you are too old to be trick-or-treating.

135
00:05:54,680 --> 00:05:56,720
And so in this case, aren't you...

136
00:05:57,940 --> 00:06:02,240
is a way to introduce something that we know is true, that we think is true.

137
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:04,540
It's kind of rude and sarcastic.

138
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:07,020
So if I say, aren't you supposed to be at work?

139
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:10,560
Oh, shoot.

140
00:06:10,560 --> 00:06:11,740
So it's in that form.

141
00:06:11,960 --> 00:06:13,720
You're not really asking a question.

142
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:16,240
You're more demanding an explanation.

143
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:17,400
Right.

144
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:18,400
It's a suggestion.

145
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:20,380
It's like, you're probably supposed to be at work right now.

146
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:25,200
So Marco, aren't you supposed to be at school right now?

147
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:26,500
So do we always...

148
00:06:26,500 --> 00:06:28,780
but we always use it in that sense, right?

149
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:29,400
Aren't you...

150
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:30,720
and then...

151
00:06:30,820 --> 00:06:31,020
Yeah.

152
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:31,480
Aren't you...

153
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:36,620
Aren't you a little old for that kind of an outfit?

154
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:37,880
All right.

155
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:39,220
So aren't you...

156
00:06:39,880 --> 00:06:40,020
All right.

157
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:40,480
Very good.

158
00:06:40,760 --> 00:06:43,360
So those are the two phrases for fluency builder today.

159
00:06:43,820 --> 00:06:47,300
Let's listen quickly to our dialogue one more time and we'll be right back.

160
00:06:57,120 --> 00:06:58,120
Trick-or-treat.

161
00:06:58,120 --> 00:07:01,120
Tom, aren't you a little too old to be trick-or-treating?

162
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:01,930
What are you talking about?

163
00:07:02,680 --> 00:07:03,550
Where's your Halloween spirit?

164
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:08,150
Didn't you ever dress up in a costume and go around the neighborhood trick-or-treating with your friends?

165
00:07:08,690 --> 00:07:09,490
Of course I did.

166
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:12,370
But when I was 10, trick-or-treating is for kids.

167
00:07:12,830 --> 00:07:16,590
Plus, I'm sure people think you're a kidnapper or something, running around with kids at night.

168
00:07:16,910 --> 00:07:17,150
Whatever.

169
00:07:17,770 --> 00:07:18,550
I'm going next door.

170
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:22,360
I heard Mrs. Robinson is giving out big bags of M&Ms.

171
00:07:22,360 --> 00:07:24,160
All right.

172
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:33,320
So, Marco, have you ever trick-or-treated?

173
00:07:33,320 --> 00:07:33,660
I did.

174
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:35,320
I did.

175
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:39,560
I used to go trick-or-treating when I was little and it was actually really fun.

176
00:07:39,560 --> 00:07:41,920
We used to have competitions about who had the most candy.

177
00:07:42,440 --> 00:07:43,240
I've heard about this.

178
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:44,440
Right?

179
00:07:44,440 --> 00:07:45,000
Because you don't necessarily bring a bag.

180
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:46,520
You bring a pillowcase.

181
00:07:46,520 --> 00:07:47,520
Right.

182
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:48,900
And you fill your pillowcase with candy.

183
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:55,660
Now, the interesting thing is that sometimes you wouldn't necessarily only get candy from people.

184
00:07:56,390 --> 00:07:56,930
Right.

185
00:07:56,930 --> 00:08:02,890
Sometimes you got weird things like pennies or toothbrushes or apples.

186
00:08:03,280 --> 00:08:04,280
Yeah.

187
00:08:04,280 --> 00:08:06,070
So you would go to someone's house and they would give you an apple.

188
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:06,870
You'd be like, okay.

189
00:08:08,280 --> 00:08:14,640
But there was always an old lady who lived near, well, she lived in the neighborhood and every neighborhood had one.

190
00:08:15,400 --> 00:08:19,460
All my friends had a lady they would go to and she gave the full-size candy bars.

191
00:08:19,740 --> 00:08:19,880
Wow.

192
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:21,800
Right?

193
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:28,720
Because normally stores sell small candy bars for the Halloween holiday because they're smaller, they're cheaper, it's easier to give them to many people.

194
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:33,800
But there's always one person who has the big full-size candy bars and everyone goes to her.

195
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:34,800
Right.

196
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:35,800
Exactly.

197
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:46,760
And actually it's amazing how Halloween is such an important event in the States because everyone dresses up, everyone puts out decorations or pumpkins outside of their homes.

198
00:08:46,940 --> 00:08:51,420
They have pumpkin carving contests which are really cool how they carve out pumpkins.

199
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:56,140
They make faces in the pumpkins and then we can put a candle inside to light it up.

200
00:08:56,360 --> 00:08:57,040
It's really fun.

201
00:08:57,480 --> 00:08:58,780
So that's called a jack-o'-lantern, right?

202
00:08:59,120 --> 00:08:59,240
That's right.

203
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:01,120
Why is it called a jack-o'-lantern?

204
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:01,280
Do you know?

205
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:03,120
I have no idea.

206
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:04,120
It's weird.

207
00:09:04,120 --> 00:09:05,120
Maybe it's like a person looks like a person's face.

208
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:06,120
Maybe a jack-o'-lantern.

209
00:09:06,120 --> 00:09:07,000
Because a lantern is a light.

210
00:09:07,720 --> 00:09:08,980
It's like a light with a candle in it.

211
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:13,340
It's actually a very fun holiday.

212
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:23,670
It's really interesting and it's kind of strange when people really oppose it because they say that it has to do with the devil or it has to do...

213
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:25,670
It's a pagan ritual and things like this.

214
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:26,750
But you know, it's just good fun.

215
00:09:26,840 --> 00:09:27,840
It's fun.

216
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:28,840
It's silly.

217
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:29,840
Everyone likes to dress up in a costume.

218
00:09:29,890 --> 00:09:33,870
What was the weirdest costume that you've dressed up as, as an adult or a child?

219
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:35,730
Because I know you've dressed up as an adult as well.

220
00:09:36,200 --> 00:09:37,090
I dress up every year.

221
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:39,280
I was Pippi Longstocking last year.

222
00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:47,120
Well, as a child one year, I dressed up as an injured soldier because my dad was in the military so we always had fatigues around the house.

223
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:51,840
And so I used his fatigues and I put blood on my face and I had crutches.

224
00:09:51,840 --> 00:09:53,960
And people thought that I really had a broken leg.

225
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:56,320
So they used to give me double...

226
00:09:56,320 --> 00:09:59,700
That year they gave me double the candy because they thought, oh, here's a poor girl on crutches.

227
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:00,700
And trick or treating.

228
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:05,200
And my costume was an injured soldier so they just didn't understand it.

229
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:05,920
It's really funny.

230
00:10:06,180 --> 00:10:06,340
Nice.

231
00:10:06,900 --> 00:10:08,320
Well, we want to hear about you guys.

232
00:10:08,520 --> 00:10:10,840
If you go trick or treating or if you dress up for Halloween.

233
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:16,700
I know that many places around the world have parties or festivities for Halloween and people dress up.

234
00:10:17,320 --> 00:10:19,200
So come to our website EnglishPod.com.

235
00:10:19,220 --> 00:10:21,720
We'll see you guys there and we'll see you guys next time.

236
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:22,500
Happy Halloween.

237
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:23,340
Happy Halloween.
